course: dictionary
lesson: 1764
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence around them tells you which meaning is right. The dictionary gives cleat three meanings. «Blind cords should be tied up high or wound on a cleat, a small hook fixed high on the wall.» The words tied and wound show that a cleat is something you wrap a cord around.
explain: Look for clues. Ask yourself: where is this happening, and what is being done? If the sentence mentions sport, grass or a match, the cleat is a stud on a shoe. If it mentions a boat, a dock or tying up a rope, it is the metal or wooden piece that the rope goes around.
explain: Words can also change from place to place. In the US and Canada, children playing soccer wear cleats. In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, people say football boots with studs. Ella in London reads an American book and must check where the story is set before she decides what cleats means.
fact: Some words have more than one meaning, and cleat is one of them.
fact: «Cleat» means one of the hard studs on the bottom of a sports shoe that stops you slipping on grass.
fact: In the US and Canada, «cleats» means sports shoes with studs, such as football or soccer boots.
fact: A cleat can also be a T-shaped piece of metal or wood on a boat or dock, which you tie a rope around.
fact: A good check is to swap the meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «She laced up her new soccer cleats before the game.» What are cleats here? | A kind of football goal | A T-shaped piece of metal for tying ropes on a boat | A small hook fixed high on a wall | *Sports shoes with studs for grass, such as football boots
exercise: «The crew tied the rope around the cleat on the dock.» What does cleat mean in this sentence? | A kind of rope knot | A stud on the bottom of a shoe | A tall pole on a ship | *A T-shaped piece of metal or wood that a rope is tied around
exercise: Which word in «The striker wore cleats to stop him slipping on the wet grass» tells you the cleats are for a sport? Copy it. | wore | wet | slipping | *striker
test: Blind cords should be tied up high or wound on a cleat. Where is the cleat most likely to be? | Inside a cup of tea | Under the sole of a sports shoe | On a football pitch, under the goal | *On a wall, as a small hook
test: Which meaning fits «He lost a cleat in the mud during the match»? | A rope knot | A small hook on a wall | A T-shaped piece on a boat | *A stud on a sports shoe
exam: Which meaning of cleat fits «The sailors tied the boat off to the cleat on the dock»? | Football boots in the US | A kind of sailing knot | *A T-shaped piece of metal or wood on a boat or dock, for tying a rope | A stud on a sports shoe
